  "text_md": "## Original Article\n\n### Incidences, Predictors, and Clinical Outcomes of Acute and Late Stent Malapposition Detected by Optical Coherence\n\n### Tomography After Drug-Eluting Stent Implantation\n\nEui Im, MD*; Byeong-Keuk Kim, MD*; Young-Guk Ko, MD; Dong-Ho Shin, MD; Jung-Sun Kim, MD; Donghoon Choi, MD; Yangsoo Jang, MD; Myeong-Ki Hong, MD, PhD\n\n***Background***—We investigated the incidences, predictors, and clinical outcomes of acute and late stent malapposition detected by optical coherence tomography (OCT) after drug-eluting stent implantation. ***Methods and Results***—We analyzed the OCT images from 351 patients with 356 lesions who received poststent and follow- up OCT examinations. Acute stent malapposition was observed in 62% of lesions. Approximately half of the acute stent Downloaded from Downloaded from Downloaded from Downloaded from Downloaded from malappositions were located within the edges of the stents. Severe diameter stenosis, calcified lesions, and long stents were independent predictors of acute stent malapposition. Follow-up OCT examinations were performed 175±60 days after drug-eluting stent implantation. Thirty-one percent of lesions with acute stent malapposition remained malapposed (late-persistent stent malapposition) and were typically (72%) located within the edges of the stent. The location within the stent edges and the volume of acute stent malapposition were independent predictors of late-persistent stent malapposition. [http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/Acute](http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/Acute) stent malapposition with a volume >2.56 mm differentiated late-persistent stent malapposition from resolved 3\n\nacute stent malapposition. Late-acquired stent malapposition was detected in 15% of all lesions and was usually (61%) located within the stent body. Late-acquired stent malapposition was more frequently associated with plaque/thrombus prolapse on poststent OCT images (70% versus 42%; *P*<0.001). Clinical events, including cardiovascular death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, and stent thrombosis, did not occur in patients with late stent malapposition during the follow-up period of 28.6±10.3 months after drug-eluting stent implantation. ***Conclusions***—Acute, late-persistent, and late-acquired stent malapposition had relatively high incidences but different predictors. The clinical outcome of stent malapposition was favorable. **(*Circ Cardiovasc Interv*. 2014;7:00-00.)**\n\n**Key Words:** coronary disease ◼ drug-eluting stents ◼ tomography, optical coherence\n\nby guest on June 16, 2017 by guest on June 16, 2017 by guest on June 16, 2017 by guest on June 16, 2017 by guest on June 16, 2017 oronary stent malapposition is the separation of ≥1 stent on detection of acute and late stent malapposition by OCT in Cstrut from the intimal surface of the coronary arterial wall small sample sizes. Therefore, we investigated the incidences, without involvement of side branches. 1 In the era of drug-predictors, and clinical outcomes of acute and late stent eluting stents (DESs), late-acquired stent malapposition is a malapposition detected by OCT in a large number of patients well-recognized problem in interventional cardiology because who received DESs. it may constitute a potent substrate for late stent thrombo- sis. 1–3 The incidence of late-acquired stent malapposition was**Methods** reported to be as high as 25% in patients with acute myocar- dial infarction. 4 In addition, predictors of late-acquired stent **Study Population** Patients who received implantation of DESs for de novo coronary malapposition include plaque or thrombus absorption or posi- lesions between January 2009 and December 2011 with poststent and tive vascular remodeling. 1,5,6 Intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) follow-up OCT were identified from the OCT registry database of our imaging was used to detect stent malapposition in previous institute. Exclusion criteria included the following (1) the DES was studies. IVUS may not completely detect stent malapposition implanted for left main coronary disease, (2) there were overlapping because of limited axial resolution (100–200 μm) or stent- DESs implanted in the lesion, (3) the clinical follow-up period after 5DES implantation was <1 year, (4) follow-up OCT was performed >1 related artifacts. However, optical coherence tomography year after DES implantation, and (5) the OCT image had poor quality. (OCT) with a higher resolution (12–18 μm) may detect stent Ultimately, 351 patients with 356 lesions were enrolled in this study. malapposition with greater accuracy. There are limited data During the same study period, there were 3235 patients (3587 lesions)\n\nReceived August 20, 2013; accepted December 15, 2013. From the Division of Cardiology, Severance Cardiovascular Hospital (E.I., B.-K.K., Y.-G.K., D.-H.S., J.-S.K., D.C., Y.J., M.-K.H.) and Severance Biomedical Science Institute (Y.J., M.-K.H.), Yonsei University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea. *Drs Im and Kim contributed equally to this work. **The Data Supplement is available at [http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/lookup/suppl/doi:10.1161/CIRCINTERVENTIONS.113.000797/-/DC1.**](http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org/lookup/suppl/doi:10.1161/CIRCINTERVENTIONS.113.000797/-/DC1.**) Correspondence to Myeong-Ki Hong, MD, PhD, Division of Cardiology, Severance Cardiovascular Hospital, Yonsei University College of Medicine, 250 Seongsanno, Seodaemun-gu, Seoul 120-752, Korea. E-mail mkhong61@yuhs.ac © 2014 American Heart Association, Inc. ***Circ Cardiovasc Interv* is available at [http://circinterventions.ahajournals.org](http://